  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 359 AM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\n- Chance for Scattered Thunderstorms Today\n\nHigh resolution models all have a signal for scattered storms\ntoday and the SPC outlook agrees with general thunder and a\nmarginal risk across the forecast area. Looking at the 24 hour max\nreflectivity from the HREF there are two main zones for convection.\nThe first is in the SPC marginal risk across Central Lower \nMichigan. Storms may both develop this morning and again this\nafternoon over Central Lower, peeling to the southeast into the \nthumb with time. The second area of focus is really just to the \nsouth and west of the area like yesterday. The RAP is showing\n1000-2000 j/kg of MUCAPE at max heating. Stronger mid level winds\nare noted over Central and Northern Lower, so if there are chances\nfor severe weather today it will likely be up in Central Lower\nwith wind being the main threat.\n\n- Saturday and Sunday is the Next Good Chance for Storms\n\nWe will largely be dry after this evening until we get to Saturday\nnight and Sunday when our next system will be working through the\nstate. A mid level shortwave looks to be the driver of this\nconvection as it tops the Central Plains ridge and moves through\nthe Great Lakes. Dew points surge into the 70s with MUCAPE values\nin excess of 2000 j/kg so stronger storms are certainly possible,\nespecially given mid level flow around 40 knots.\n\n- Seasonable Temperatures Expected\n\nNo major changes in temperature are expected. Highs look to be in\nthe 80s right through the 7 day forecast.",
